data.content:
Here's what I did — the **lightest** fix for .NET 8+ is to use the built-in `TimeProvider` class (no new packages, no DI container needed):

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _timeProvider = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**Why this works without DI:**
- The `= null` default means existing callers (`new InvoiceCalculator()`) keep working unchanged.
- In production it falls back to `TimeProvider.System` (real clock).

**In tests**, use `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`:

```
dotnet add <TestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 3, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 28, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 28, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

Apply the same pattern to your other classes that read `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`.
